WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.744
Picture a café owner who was paid 12.40 too little, while every program reported success.

2
00:00:09.033 --> 00:00:18.363
One way to catch that: work the balances out again from the log, the list of every payment, and compare them with the live ones, account by account.

3
00:00:18.633 --> 00:00:19.815
Now make it real.

4
00:00:19.815 --> 00:00:24.748
The live view has 50 million rows, in the database that serves the app.

5
00:00:25.033 --> 00:00:31.035
The rebuilt view sits in the warehouse, a separate database kept for analysis, on other machines.

6
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:33.120
Somewhere one balance is off.

7
00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:37.701
How do you find it without sending fifty million rows across the network?

8
00:00:37.701 --> 00:00:38.893
Hold that question.

9
00:00:39.167 --> 00:00:41.075
The plain way is row by row.

10
00:00:41.075 --> 00:00:46.049
Every check sends all fifty million rows across, to find one bad balance.

11
00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:52.925
That's two libraries checking they hold the same books by reading every page to each other over the phone.

12
00:00:52.925 --> 00:00:53.737
Bring snacks.

13
00:00:54.033 --> 00:00:59.843
What we want is a fingerprint for data: something short that the two sides can compare instead.

14
00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:03.646
There's a tool for that, called a hash function.

15
00:01:03.646 --> 00:01:05.788
SHA-256 is a widely used one.

16
00:01:06.067 --> 00:01:12.153
It turns any amount of data into a short number of a fixed size, 32 bytes, called its hash.

17
00:01:12.433 --> 00:01:15.091
The same data always gives the same hash.

18
00:01:15.091 --> 00:01:18.591
Change even one bit, and the hash comes out different.

19
00:01:18.833 --> 00:01:20.307
So, the obvious plan.

20
00:01:20.307 --> 00:01:23.886
Each side hashes its whole view and sends one hash.

21
00:01:23.886 --> 00:01:24.728
They differ.

22
00:01:24.728 --> 00:01:26.412
Good, something's wrong.

23
00:01:26.667 --> 00:01:27.079
Where?

24
00:01:27.079 --> 00:01:28.386
One hash can't say.

25
00:01:28.386 --> 00:01:31.826
It's a smoke alarm that won't tell you which room.

26
00:01:31.826 --> 00:01:34.096
We're back to fifty million rows.

27
00:01:34.367 --> 00:01:37.148
Back to the two libraries on the phone.

28
00:01:37.148 --> 00:01:40.786
A smarter pair asks: is the whole library the same?

29
00:01:40.786 --> 00:01:41.000
No.

30
00:01:41.233 --> 00:01:42.543
The east wing?

31
00:01:42.543 --> 00:01:44.134
Same, so skip it.

32
00:01:44.134 --> 00:01:45.444
The west wing?

33
00:01:45.444 --> 00:01:46.380
Different.

34
00:01:46.467 --> 00:01:50.452
Then its rooms, then their shelves, until they're down to one shelf.

35
00:01:50.452 --> 00:01:53.733
Only then do they read books aloud, from that one shelf.

36
00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:54.905
Let's map it.

37
00:01:54.905 --> 00:01:56.577
The library is the view.

38
00:01:56.577 --> 00:01:58.458
A shelf is a range of keys.

39
00:01:58.458 --> 00:02:02.219
A shelf's fingerprint is the hash of everything on it.

40
00:02:02.467 --> 00:02:07.121
A room's fingerprint is a hash of its shelves' fingerprints, and so on up.

41
00:02:07.121 --> 00:02:09.008
The phone line is the network.

42
00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:10.736
Let's build it small.

43
00:02:10.736 --> 00:02:13.335
Each side cuts its keys into 8 ranges.

44
00:02:13.335 --> 00:02:18.259
A key's range comes from a hash of the key, so the ranges come out even.

45
00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:23.030
Each leaf is the hash of every entry in its range, in key order.

46
00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:24.517
Live on top, in blue.

47
00:02:24.517 --> 00:02:26.499
Rebuilt underneath, in gold.

48
00:02:26.733 --> 00:02:32.756
Above them, each parent is the hash of its two children, level by level, up to a single root.

49
00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:34.440
That's a Merkle tree.

50
00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:37.664
Each side builds its own, where its data lives.

51
00:02:37.933 --> 00:02:40.258
One balance is off by a flipped bit.

52
00:02:40.258 --> 00:02:43.811
How many comparisons to find its range among the eight?

53
00:02:43.811 --> 00:02:44.844
Pause and guess.

54
00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:49.611
Compare the roots.

55
00:02:49.611 --> 00:02:50.640
They differ.

56
00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:51.927
One comparison.

57
00:02:52.167 --> 00:02:54.233
Compare the root's two children.

58
00:02:54.233 --> 00:03:00.369
The left one matches, so the first four ranges are the same, and nothing below it needs a look.

59
00:03:00.369 --> 00:03:01.790
The right one differs.

60
00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:02.177
Three.

61
00:03:02.433 --> 00:03:05.631
Under it, left matches, right differs.

62
00:03:05.631 --> 00:03:06.052
Five.

63
00:03:06.333 --> 00:03:10.075
At the bottom, range six differs and range seven matches.

64
00:03:10.075 --> 00:03:13.685
Seven comparisons, and only hashes crossed the network.

65
00:03:13.933 --> 00:03:19.337
Now, and only now, compare rows, only inside range six: an eighth of the keys.

66
00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:21.761
Each level costs two comparisons.

67
00:03:22.033 --> 00:03:33.162
So with 1,024 ranges, ten levels deep, it's one plus two times ten: 21 comparisons, instead of 1,024 ranges, or fifty million rows.

68
00:03:33.162 --> 00:03:37.919
And it leaves about a thousandth of the rows to compare.

69
00:03:38.167 --> 00:03:42.161
So more ranges are always better, and the tree always wins?

70
00:03:42.161 --> 00:03:43.108
Let's be fair.

71
00:03:43.108 --> 00:03:47.915
The cost grows with the number of bad ranges, not the size of the view.

72
00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:55.447
In the lesson's lab, with 256 ranges, one bad account took 17 comparisons.

73
00:03:55.447 --> 00:03:56.721
Ten took 101.

74
00:03:56.721 --> 00:04:03.772
A hundred took 369: more than the 256 ranges a plain scan would compare.

75
00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:06.960
When most ranges differ, a plain scan is cheaper.

76
00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:09.679
And then you don't have a hundred small bugs.

77
00:04:09.679 --> 00:04:13.364
You have one big one, and a few ranges are enough to find it.

78
00:04:13.633 --> 00:04:17.736
If the whole library is flooded, nobody checks it shelf by shelf.

79
00:04:18.133 --> 00:04:21.692
Could two different ranges share a hash by chance?

80
00:04:21.692 --> 00:04:25.677
With SHA-256, the odds are far too small to worry about.

81
00:04:25.967 --> 00:04:29.240
So, how do you find one bad row in fifty million?

82
00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:33.383
Exchange hashes, not rows, and go down only where they differ.

83
00:04:33.633 --> 00:04:38.120
Git, which keeps a project's code history, uses the same kind of tree.

84
00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:39.627
One quick check.

85
00:04:39.627 --> 00:04:41.037
The two roots match.

86
00:04:41.037 --> 00:04:43.714
How many more comparisons do you need?

87
00:04:43.714 --> 00:04:44.841
Pause and think.

88
00:04:48.033 --> 00:04:48.438
None.

89
00:04:48.438 --> 00:04:50.785
Equal roots mean equal views.

90
00:04:50.785 --> 00:04:53.941
The audit is done after one comparison.
